                    clock_r4:               `Defend the four-metric scorecard · renew 31 seats`,

                    round_name_r1:          `Diagnose`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Rebalance ESG & Core`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Balance the Regions`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Defend the Design`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: integration, not addition`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you touch a single hour, name the problem correctly. The sponsors' letter is not a request for <em>more</em> ESG content — it is a verdict that ESG sits <strong>bolted on</strong> while the three regions feel incoherent. Your last cohort scored +47 but wrote “ESG felt bolted on” and “too much overlap.” The trap is to read this as “add a Friday ESG block” when the real flaw is design: ESG must live <strong>inside</strong> finance, strategy and operations. State the principle that will govern the redesign — and be honest that this is an <em>integration</em> problem, not an <em>addition</em> one.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Rebalance ESG and the core inside 480 hours`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The schedule is locked at <strong>480 hours</strong> — every hour you add to ESG is an hour taken from the core or the regions. Today's split is 320 core / 70 ESG / 90 regional. Lift ESG to a <strong>credible</strong> level (sponsors will read a token 70-ish as a refusal) without cutting the 4.6/5 core so hard that your <em>NPS falls through the +40 floor</em>. Then decide how ESG is <strong>delivered</strong> — integrated, blended or a visible standalone block — and, critically, how it is <strong>assessed</strong>. Hours on a timetable are exposure; a graded deliverable is proof of capability.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Balance three residencies into one journey`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Mid-design shock: São Paulo faculty resist losing finance hours, and a Singapore partner dangles a “free” sponsored operations residency that would tilt the programme east. The current 210/150/120 split is flagged as incoherent. Rebalance the regions toward a <strong>coherent global journey</strong> — each city contributing a distinct strength — rather than chasing free hours. Decide the sponsored residency <em>on terms that protect balance</em>, design a connective <strong>thread</strong> that makes three trips feel like one programme, and manage the faculty resistance so the redesign survives contact with the regions.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Lock the design & defend the scorecard`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Four-minute pitch to the lead corporate sponsor and the academic dean. They will probe whether ESG is <strong>assessed or merely scheduled</strong>, whether the core stays employer-relevant, and whether satisfaction holds. Commit to a <strong>four-metric scorecard</strong> — global balance, ESG coverage, satisfaction AND employer relevance — show <em>assessed</em> integration with rubrics rather than pointing at hours, and name exactly what you cut and the principle that decided it. Do this and the <strong>31 sponsored seats (€4.05M)</strong> renew.`,

                    r1_principle_label:     `What design principle will govern the redesign?`,
                    prin_integrate:         `Integrate ESG into finance, strategy and operations — embedded, not appended`,
                    prin_addmodule:         `Add a visible standalone ESG module so sponsors can count the hours`,
                    prin_protectcore:       `Protect the 4.6/5 core above all — give ESG the minimum to keep sponsors quiet`,
                    prin_accumulate:        `Add everything everyone wants — find the hours later`,
                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `Where is the single highest-leverage design flaw today?`,
                    diag_boltedon:          `ESG sits as a disconnected bolt-on while the regions feel incoherent`,
                    diag_notenough:         `There simply isn't enough ESG content yet — just add more hours`,
                    diag_corequality:       `The core management content isn't strong enough`,
                    diag_nothing:           `Nothing structural — the ranking will recover on its own`,
                    r1_candor_label:        `How candidly does your team name this as an integration problem, not an addition one? (%)`,
                    r1_candor_readout:      `Willingness to redesign for integration, not just add hours:`,

