                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_integrate:     `<strong>That is the design principle the sponsors are actually asking for.</strong> They want leaders who can do ESG inside finance, strategy and operations — not a module they can count. Integration is harder to evidence than a Friday block, but it is the only answer that survives the “ESG felt bolted on” comment.`,
                    coach_r1_addmodule:     `<strong>A standalone ESG module is the seductive wrong answer.</strong> Sponsors can see the hours — but your last cohort already wrote “ESG felt bolted on,” and now there is more of it, still disconnected. You meet the letter and miss the point. Where does climate risk show up <em>inside</em> your finance and strategy cases?`,
                    coach_r1_protectcore:   `<strong>Protecting the core at all costs misreads the leverage.</strong> Three sponsors funding 31 seats — €4.05M — asked for <em>measurable</em> integration. Giving ESG the minimum reads as a refusal. Which is the bigger risk: a slightly leaner core, or losing the seats?`,
                    coach_r1_accumulate:    `<strong>“Add everything, find the hours later” is not a design.</strong> The schedule is fixed at 480 hours; accumulation guarantees you blow the cap in Round 2. Decide the principle first, then let it make the cuts for you.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the principle as integration AND located the real flaw — ESG bolted on while the regions feel incoherent. That is exactly the lens that lets you redesign within 480 hours instead of just stacking more on top.`,
                    coach_r1_notenough:     `<strong>“Just add more ESG hours” is the addition trap.</strong> The complaint was never volume — it was that ESG felt disconnected. More disconnected hours is more of the same problem. The fix is integration, not quantity.`,
                    coach_r1_corequality:   `<strong>The core is not the problem.</strong> Participants rate it 4.6/5 and employers resist losing it. Diagnosing the core as weak points you at the wrong cut and risks the satisfaction floor. The flaw is how ESG and the regions are designed, not the core's quality.`,
                    coach_r1_nothing:       `<strong>“The ranking will recover on its own” is how you lose 31 seats.</strong> The sponsors put a contractual condition in writing. Doing nothing structural is precisely the inaction that triggers single-year-only funding. There is a real, fixable design flaw.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>This is how a redesign becomes window-dressing.</strong> Low willingness to call it an integration problem means you will add a module and call it a plan — and repeat last cohort's “bolted-on” verdict. Name the real flaw honestly.`,
                    coach_r2_overcap:       `<strong>Your curriculum is brilliant — and it is over 480 hours.</strong> These are working executives; accreditation and their employers both say 480. You didn't design, you accumulated. What comes out, and what principle decides?`,
                    coach_r2_undercap:      `<strong>You left hours on the table.</strong> Under 480 wastes scarce contact time these executives are paying for. Use the full envelope deliberately — empty hours are not the same as restraint.`,
                    coach_r2_esgcredible:   `<strong>That is a credible, sponsor-readable ESG commitment.</strong> Lifting ESG into the ~110–150 band signals genuine integration without gutting the core. Now make sure it is delivered inside the cases and actually assessed.`,
                    coach_r2_esgoverload:   `<strong>This is a lot of ESG — possibly too much.</strong> You have cleared the sponsor bar, but pushing ESG this high starts squeezing the core that participants rate 4.6/5. Watch the satisfaction floor; integration, not sheer volume, is what they asked for.`,
                    coach_r2_esgtoken:      `<strong>That is barely more ESG than today.</strong> 70-ish hours after a written ultimatum reads as a refusal to the three sponsors funding 31 seats. They asked for <em>measurable</em> integration — give them a credible level, not a token.`,
                    coach_r2_corecut:       `<strong>You have cut the 4.6/5 core hard.</strong> Hit the ESG target by gutting the content participants love and your projected NPS slides toward — or through — the +40 floor. You solved for the sponsor and broke the participant. Protect both inside 480 hours.`,
                    coach_r2_integrated:    `<strong>Integrated delivery is the pedagogically strong choice.</strong> ESG living inside the finance, strategy and operations cases is exactly what “integration, not addition” means — harder to evidence, so pair it with a graded deliverable.`,
                    coach_r2_standalone:    `<strong>A standalone block is visible but bolted on.</strong> Sponsors can point to it, but participants will feel it as the disconnected ESG they already complained about. Visibility is not integration.`,
                    coach_r2_assessed:      `<strong>Integrated AND assessed — that is the gold standard.</strong> ESG inside core cases with a graded deliverable proves participants can <em>do</em> ESG, not just sit through it. This is the evidence the sponsor will demand in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r2_noassess:      `<strong>Attendance is not learning.</strong> Counting hours scheduled with no assessment is exactly the “exposure, not capability” trap. How will you prove the sponsor's people are more ESG-<em>capable</em>, not just more ESG-<em>exposed</em>?`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>That is a coherent global balance.</strong> Three residencies near parity, each contributing a distinct strength, reads as one programme rather than three competing trips. Now bind them with a connective thread.`,
                    coach_r3_lopsided:      `<strong>This split tilts the programme.</strong> One region dwarfing the others recreates the incoherence participants already flagged. Coherence comes from balance plus a thread, not from loading hours into one city.`,
                    coach_r3_terms:         `<strong>Accepting on terms is the disciplined move.</strong> You take the sponsored residency's value without letting it tilt the programme east. The gift serves the design, not the other way round.`,
                    coach_r3_grab:          `<strong>The free operations residency is tempting — but it is not free.</strong> Now Asia-Pacific holds a disproportionate share and the programme tilts east. You optimised for a sponsor's gift, not for a balanced global leader. One coherent journey, or three competing ones?`,
                    coach_r3_journey:       `<strong>Now it is one journey.</strong> A balanced split, the residency taken on terms, and an integrated capstone running through all three cities — that is three residencies designed as a single global-leadership arc, not three disconnected trips.`,
                    coach_r3_nothread:      `<strong>Three strong residencies are still three trips without a thread.</strong> Coherence does not happen by itself — participants flagged exactly this incoherence. A connective capstone is what makes the cities add up to one programme.`,
                    coach_r3_faculty:       `<strong>You are pushing a redesign past resistant faculty.</strong> Cutting São Paulo's finance hours without co-design breeds quiet sabotage and erodes the participant experience. Engage the regional faculty or the design won't survive contact.`,
                    coach_r4_fourmetric:    `<strong>A four-metric scorecard is the honest, defensible commitment.</strong> Global balance, ESG coverage, satisfaction AND employer relevance — you are promising to be judged on the whole design, not just the renewal condition. That is what earns sponsor trust.`,
                    coach_r4_esgonly:       `<strong>An ESG-only scorecard is narrow and brittle.</strong> It satisfies the letter of the condition but signals you will sacrifice balance and satisfaction to hit one number. The dean will hear a programme out of balance.`,
                    coach_r4_satisfaction:  `<strong>A satisfaction-only scorecard dodges the renewal condition.</strong> Keeping participants happy while quietly under-delivering on ESG is exactly what the sponsors wrote the letter to stop. You will protect NPS and lose the seats.`,
                    coach_r4_assessed:      `<strong>Assessed integration with rubrics is the proof that renews seats.</strong> You showed the sponsor graded ESG deliverables inside core cases — capability, not a calendar. That is the difference between exposure and learning they were probing for.`,
                    coach_r4_hours:         `<strong>Pointing at the timetable is exposure, not evidence.</strong> ESG hours on a calendar prove nothing a participant can <em>do</em>. The sponsor asked whether it is assessed or merely scheduled — the timetable answers the wrong question.`,
                    coach_r4_intent:        `<strong>A statement of intent is a promise, not proof.</strong> “Assessment comes next year” is precisely the bolt-on deferral that lost trust last time. The sponsors funding 31 seats want capability now, not a roadmap.`,
                    coach_r4_overlap:       `<strong>Naming the exact cut and the principle behind it is leadership.</strong> Pointing to the duplicated core hours you removed — and the integration principle that decided it — shows you designed within the constraint rather than dodged it.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>“We cut nothing, we fit it all in” does not survive scrutiny.</strong> Inside a hard 480-hour cap, claiming zero sacrifice means you either blew the cap or are not being straight. The honest answer names the cut and the principle.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
